  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...A strong cold front will bring the next round \nof showers and thunderstorms Tuesday through Tuesday night. Some\nstorms may be severe.\n\nAn active Tuesday is expected as an unseasonably strong mid level \ntrough digs into the Great Lakes, with mid level height falls, DPVA, \nand upper level divergence ahead of the trough all contributing to a \nbackground of large scale ascent. The airmass today is quite dry, \nbut increasing southwesterly flow ahead of the trough will transport \nimproved moisture into the region by midday Tuesday, with surface \ndewpoints rising to the mid to upper 60s. Strong low/mid level flow \nby mid summer standards will cross the eastern Great Lakes, \nsupporting moderate to strong shear profiles.\n\nWhile the large scale setup is certainly favorable for severe \nthunderstorms, high-res CAMS guidance continues to suggest a messy \nstorm evolution. Debris clouds and scattered showers will likely \ncross the region in the morning from decaying upstream convection. \nIf this persists long enough, it may restrict destabilization to \nsome extent. Initial SSW flow will gradually veer to the SW through \nthe day, with stable lake shadows expanding northeast of the lakes \nand diminishing instability across the Niagara Frontier and \nJefferson County. \n\nConvective initiation will likely occur by midday to early afternoon \nalong 1) a strengthening lake breeze boundary from the Southern Tier \ninto the Finger Lakes and 2) possibly an outflow boundary and/or \nMCV from leftover upstream convection moving into the area from the \nwest. If the outflow boundary or MCV ends up being the main focus \nfor convection, that could potentially overcome the negative impacts \nof stable lake shadows. Still, the most likely location of severe \nweather and best coverage of showers and thunderstorms in general \nwill be Tuesday afternoon and early evening from the Southern Tier \ninto the Finger Lakes and Central NY, away from the stabilizing \ninfluence of the lakes.\n\nThe shear/instability profiles will support multiple modes of \nconvection including multicell clusters, bowing segments, and \nsupercells. The primary risk will be isolated damaging wind gusts in \nthe stronger, more sustained storms. If bowing segments develop, \ncorridors of wind damage are possible. Forecast soundings show more \nfavorably curved hodographs and high effective SRH values across the \nSouthern Tier and Finger Lakes, where an isolated tornado is \npossible. Storm motion will be quite fast given the strong wind \nfields, but storms will produce brief, torrential downpours with a \nlow risk of isolated flash flooding if training occurs along lake \nbreeze boundaries.\n\nThe actual cold front will not cross the area until Tuesday evening, \nand may produce another line of showers and scattered thunderstorms. \nCoverage of showers and especially thunder will diminish by mid to \nlate evening with the loss of diurnal instability. \n\nKey Message 2...Cool Wednesday, with a chance of a few lingering \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms.\n\nThe digging trough will briefly develop into a mid level closed low \nover western Quebec Wednesday. Deep moisture will be east of the \narea by Wednesday morning, but forcing for ascent from several \nshortwaves rounding the base of the trough coupled with cool air \naloft will still support a few scattered showers through the day, \nand possibly an isolated thunderstorm or two. Both intensity and \ncoverage will be far less than Tuesday. \n\nTemperatures will be quite cool Wednesday, close to 10F below \naverage for mid summer. Highs will struggle to reach the lower 70s \nfor lower elevations, with mid 60s across higher terrain. Lows \nWednesday night will drop back into the 50s in most areas, with mid \n40s for the Southern Tier valleys and Lewis County.",
